## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design end-to-end verification systems, key management infrastructure, and incident response procedures within a security-critical fintech environment.

## Key Concepts

- JWKS-based key rotation with `kid` header identification
- Claims validation (`iss`, `aud`, `exp`, `iat`) for token integrity
- Replay protection via nonce enforcement and timestamp windows
- Webhook signing strategies (RS256 vs HS256) and their trade-offs
- Incident response protocols and key revocation processes

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo verification sketch
function verifyWebhook(token, jwksUrl, expectedIssuer, expectedAudience) {
  // Decode JWT header to get kid
  const header = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(token.split('.')[0], 'base64'));
  const kid = header.kid;

  // Fetch JWKS and find matching key
  const jwks = await fetch(jwksUrl).then(r => r.json());
  const publicKey = jwks.keys.find(k => k.kid === kid);

  // Verify JWT signature and claims
  const decoded = jwt.verify(token, publicKey, {
    issuer: expectedIssuer,
    audience: expectedAudience,
    algorithms: ['RS256']
  });

  // Validate nonce and replay protection
  if (await isNonceUsed(decoded.nonce)) {
    throw new Error('Replay attack detected');
  }

  return decoded;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical, beginner-friendly security hygiene around logging, a common real-world risk.

## Key Concepts
- Redaction in middleware
- Structured logging with masking
- Access controls on log sinks
- CI gates for secrets leakage

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example Express middleware to redact sensitive fields in req.body and req.query
function redactLogs(req, res, next) {
  const sensitive = ['email','password','token','authorization','ssn','user_id'];
  const redact = (obj) => {
    if (!obj) return obj;
    if (typeof obj !== 'object') return obj;
    for (const k of Object.keys(obj)) {
      if (sensitive.includes(k)) obj[k] = 'REDACTED';
      else if (typeof obj[k] === 'object') redact(obj[k]);
    }
    return obj;
  };
  req.redacted = redact(Object.assign({}, req.body, req.query));
  next();
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test log redaction across nested payloads?
- What changes if you switch to a distributed log system across services?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to translate a security incident into a comprehensive, end-to-end remediation plan that spans logging hygiene, secrets management, memory safety, access controls, and incident response.

## Key Concepts

- Secrets lifecycle management with short lease expirations
- Logging redaction and tamper-evident logging
- In-memory secret handling and secure memory scrubbing
- Per-tenant isolation and least-privilege access controls
- Incident response playbooks, rollback procedures, and post-mortem hardening

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: redact secrets in logs
function redactSecrets(obj) {
  const SECRET_PATTERNS = [/^(api[_-]?key|secret|token|password)$/i, /vault/i];

  return JSON.stringify(obj, (key, value) => {
    if (SECRET_PATTERNS.some(p => p.test(key))) {
      return '[REDACTED]';
    }
    return value;
  });
}

// Example: secure memory scrubbing
function clearSecretBuffer(buffer) {
  if (buffer && buffer.fill) {
    buffer.fill(0);
  }
}

// Example: tamper-evident log entry
function createLogEntry(secretKey, event, previousHash) {
  const timestamp = Date.now();
  const payload = `${timestamp}|${event}|${secretKey}|${previousHash}`;
  const hash = crypto.createHmac('sha256', process.env.LOG_SECRET)
    .update(payload)
    .digest('hex');

  return { timestamp, event, hash, previousHash };
}
```

